# Threat modeling

Method: `methodology/THREAT-MODELING.md`. Output:
`.security/threat-model.yaml` (schema: `schemas/threat-model.schema.json`)
plus narrative `.security/THREAT-MODEL.md`.

- Derive in order: architecture → assets → actors → trust boundaries →
  attacker capabilities → threats → requirements. Do not start from a generic
  vulnerability list.
- Default attacker: multiple accounts, attacking contracts, transaction
  ordering, flashloan-scale capital. Weakening this needs written
  justification.
- Every threat: a concrete attack narrative with severity
  (`specification/SEVERITY.yaml`) judged by impact, linked to assets/actors by
  ID. IDs (AST/ACT/TB/THR/REQ) are permanent — never renumber.
- Every high+ threat must end in requirements → invariants → tests or an
  explicit `accepted_risk` with a named human approver. You cannot approve
  accepted risks yourself.
- Validate your work: `security-check SEC-PROC-001` then `SEC-PROC-003`.
- When code architecture changes (new contract, new role, new external
  dependency), the threat model updates FIRST, then the code review.
